Here is a complete implementation of cacheReceiveThumb that avoids storing duplicate thumbnails by hashing the thumbnail data (SHA-256) and using the hash as the cache key. It writes the incoming stream to a temporary file while computing the digest (so it does not keep the whole image in memory), and then moves the temp file into a cache directory under a name based on the hash only if that hash is not already present.

Notes/assumptions:
- A thumbnail is considered duplicate if its content bytes are identical (checked by SHA-256).
- The cache directory is System.getProperty("java.io.tmpdir") + "/thumbCache".
- If fileInfo.getName() is available and has a known image extension, that extension is preserved; otherwise ".thumb" is used.
- The method closes the provided InputStream.
- The method throws IOException for I/O problems (as requested).

Code:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.nio.file.StandardCopyOption;
import java.security.DigestInputStream;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;

public static void cacheReceiveThumb(BaseFileInfo fileInfo, InputStream inputStream) throws IOException {
    if (fileInfo == null || inputStream == null) {
        return;
    }

    // Prepare cache directory
    Path cacheDir = Paths.get(System.getProperty("java.io.tmpdir"), "thumbCache");
    Files.createDirectories(cacheDir);

    MessageDigest md;
    try {
        md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
    } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
        // SHA-256 should always be available; wrap as IOException to match signature
        throw new IOException("SHA-256 MessageDigest not available", e);
    }

    // Write incoming stream to a temporary file while computing the digest
    Path tmpFile = Files.createTempFile(cacheDir, "thumb-", ".tmp");
    try (DigestInputStream dis = new DigestInputStream(new BufferedInputStream(inputStream), md);
         OutputStream os = Files.newOutputStream(tmpFile, StandardOpenOption.WRITE, StandardOpenOption.TRUNCATE_EXISTING)) {

        byte[] buffer = new byte[8192];
        int read;
        while ((read = dis.read(buffer)) != -1) {
            os.write(buffer, 0, read);
        }
    } catch (IOException ioe) {
        // Clean up temp file on error and rethrow
        Files.deleteIfExists(tmpFile);
        throw ioe;
    } finally {
        // Ensure inputStream is closed (DigestInputStream wrapper already closed it above)
        try {
            inputStream.close();
        } catch (IOException ignored) { }
    }

    // Compute hex digest
    byte[] digest = md.digest();
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(digest.length * 2);
    for (byte b : digest) {
        sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));
    }
    String hex = sb.toString();

    // Determine extension if possible (preserve common image extensions)
    String ext = ".thumb";
    try {
        String name = fileInfo.getName(); // assume getter exists; if not, will throw/return null
        if (name != null) {
            String lower = name.toLowerCase();
            if (lower.endsWith(".png")) ext = ".png";
            else if (lower.endsWith(".jpg") || lower.endsWith(".jpeg")) ext = ".jpg";
            else if (lower.endsWith(".gif")) ext = ".gif";
            else if (lower.endsWith(".bmp")) ext = ".bmp";
            // otherwise keep default .thumb
        }
    } catch (Exception ignored) { /* don't fail on metadata access */ }

    Path finalPath = cacheDir.resolve(hex + ext);

    // If identical image already in cache, delete temp file and do nothing
    if (Files.exists(finalPath)) {
        Files.deleteIfExists(tmpFile);
        return;
    }

    // Try atomic move; if not supported, perform a regular move/replace
    try {
        Files.move(tmpFile, finalPath, StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE);
    } catch (AtomicMoveNotSupportedException amnse) {
        Files.move(tmpFile, finalPath, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
    }
}
```

This implementation guarantees that for identical thumbnail byte sequences only a single cached file (keyed by SHA-256) will be stored.

Here’s a simple JavaScript example that alters the array’s 2nd and 3rd elements, then calculates and returns their sum:

```javascript
const array = [1, 2, 3, 4];

// Alter the 2nd and 3rd elements (indices 1 and 2)
array[1] = 5; // now array is [1, 5, 3, 4]
array[2] = 7; // now array is [1, 5, 7, 4]

// Calculate the sum of the 2nd and 3rd elements
const sum = array[1] + array[2];

console.log(sum); // 12
// or return sum from a function:
function sumSecondAndThird(arr) {
  return arr[1] + arr[2];
}
console.log(sumSecondAndThird(array)); // 12
```

Notes:
- Array indices start at 0, so the “second” element is index 1 and the “third” is index 2.
- Replace 5 and 7 with any values you want when altering the array.

Idea and reasoning

- We want the largest j - i such that i <= j and nums1[i] <= nums2[j].
- Both arrays are non-increasing (i.e., sorted descending or equal), so:
  - As i increases, nums1[i] does not increase.
  - As j increases, nums2[j] does not increase.
- Use two pointers i and j, both starting at 0. Maintain the invariant that j never moves left:
  - If nums1(i) <= nums2(j), then (i, j) is valid; record distance j - i and try to increase j to see if we can get a larger distance.
  - Otherwise nums1(i) > nums2(j), so with the current j no pair with this i will be valid; increase i to try a smaller nums1[i].
- Because j only increases and i only increases, each pointer moves at most length of its array — O(n1 + n2) time. O(1) extra space.

This algorithm finds, for each i, the farthest j (using the advancing j pointer) that satisfies the condition, so it's correct.

Complexity
- Time: O(nums1.length + nums2.length)
- Space: O(1)

Scala implementation

```scala
object Solution {
  def maxDistance(nums1: Array[Int], nums2: Array[Int]): Int = {
    var i = 0
    var j = 0
    var ans = 0
    val n1 = nums1.length
    val n2 = nums2.length

    while (i < n1 && j < n2) {
      if (nums1(i) <= nums2(j)) {
        // (i, j) is valid; update answer and try to increase j for a larger distance
        ans = Math.max(ans, j - i)
        j += 1
      } else {
        // nums1(i) > nums2(j): for this i no current j or smaller j works, advance i
        i += 1
      }
    }

    ans
  }

  // Example usage
  def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {
    val a1 = Array(55, 30, 5, 4, 2)
    val b1 = Array(100, 20, 10, 10, 5)
    println(maxDistance(a1, b1)) // 2

    val a2 = Array(2, 2, 2)
    val b2 = Array(10, 10, 1)
    println(maxDistance(a2, b2)) // 1

    val a3 = Array(30, 29, 19, 5)
    val b3 = Array(25, 25, 25, 25, 25)
    println(maxDistance(a3, b3)) // 2
  }
}
```

This follows the two-pointer approach and returns 0 when no valid pair exists.

Solution idea (brief):

- We cannot see the whole grid, so first explore (map) the reachable part of the hidden grid using DFS while controlling the robot through the GridMaster API.
- Represent the starting cell as coordinate (0,0). When we move to a neighbor we assign it coordinates relative to start (e.g. up = (-1,0), right=(0,1), ...).
- During DFS: for every direction, use master.canMove(dir) to check if we can go there. If yes, call master.move(dir) to actually move (it returns the cost to enter that cell). Record that cost and recursively continue DFS from that neighbor. After the recursive call, backtrack by moving in the opposite direction so the robot returns to the current cell (so we can explore other directions).
- While exploring record whether we found the target and the coordinates of the target.
- After DFS finishes we have a map of all reachable cells and the cost to ENTER each cell (the starting cell has no entering cost). Now run Dijkstra on the discovered graph where an edge from u to v has weight equal to the cost of v (because moving into v costs grid[v]).
- If the target is not discovered during DFS return -1; otherwise return the shortest distance found by Dijkstra.

This approach is standard for interactive hidden-grid problems: DFS to discover and build a weighted graph, Dijkstra to compute the shortest path on that discovered graph.

Rust implementation

Note: LeetCode's interactive interface names vary across languages. In Rust the method name move is a keyword; in the code below the trait defines a method named move via a raw identifier (r#move). If the judge's signature differs, adjust method names to match the platform's trait.

```rust
use std::collections::{BinaryHeap, HashMap, HashSet};
use std::cmp::Reverse;

// Example trait to illustrate usage. On the judge, the trait is provided by the platform.
// The move method is written using a raw identifier r#move because `move` is a Rust keyword.
pub trait GridMaster {
    fn canMove(&self, dir: char) -> bool;
    fn r#move(&mut self, dir: char) -> i32; // corresponds to .move(dir) in other languages
    fn isTarget(&self) -> bool;
}

pub struct Solution {}

impl Solution {
    pub fn find_shortest_path(master: &mut dyn GridMaster) -> i32 {
        // Directions: char, dx, dy, opposite char
        let dirs: [(char, i32, i32, char); 4] = [
            ('U', -1, 0, 'D'),
            ('D', 1, 0, 'U'),
            ('L', 0, -1, 'R'),
            ('R', 0, 1, 'L'),
        ];

        // cost to ENTER a cell at coordinate (x,y). Start (0,0) has no entering cost recorded.
        let mut cost_map: HashMap<(i32, i32), i32> = HashMap::new();
        let mut visited: HashSet<(i32, i32)> = HashSet::new();
        let mut target_pos: Option<(i32, i32)> = None;

        // DFS to explore reachable cells and build cost_map.
        fn dfs(
            master: &mut dyn GridMaster,
            x: i32,
            y: i32,
            visited: &mut HashSet<(i32, i32)>,
            cost_map: &mut HashMap<(i32, i32), i32>,
            target_pos: &mut Option<(i32, i32)>,
            dirs: &[(char, i32, i32, char); 4],
        ) {
            visited.insert((x, y));
            if master.isTarget() {
                *target_pos = Some((x, y));
            }
            for &(dir, dx, dy, opp) in dirs.iter() {
                let nx = x + dx;
                let ny = y + dy;
                if visited.contains(&(nx, ny)) {
                    continue;
                }
                if master.canMove(dir) {
                    // actually move
                    let c = master.r#move(dir);
                    if c == -1 {
                        // shouldn't happen when canMove is true, but guard anyway
                        continue;
                    }
                    // record cost to enter neighbor
                    cost_map.insert((nx, ny), c);
                    // recurse from neighbor (robot is now at neighbor)
                    dfs(master, nx, ny, visited, cost_map, target_pos, dirs);
                    // backtrack to current cell
                    let back = master.r#move(opp);
                    // back should succeed and return the cost of the cell we moved into (the current cell),
                    // but we don't need to use that value.
                    let _ = back;
                }
            }
        }

        // Start DFS from (0,0)
        dfs(
            master,
            0,
            0,
            &mut visited,
            &mut cost_map,
            &mut target_pos,
            &dirs,
        );

        // If target not found during exploration, return -1
        let target = match target_pos {
            Some(t) => t,
            None => return -1,
        };

        // Dijkstra on discovered graph. Nodes are positions in visited. Moving from u to v costs cost_map[v]
        let mut dist: HashMap<(i32, i32), i32> = HashMap::new();
        let mut heap: BinaryHeap<(Reverse<i32>, (i32, i32))> = BinaryHeap::new();

        dist.insert((0, 0), 0);
        heap.push((Reverse(0), (0, 0)));

        while let Some((Reverse(d), pos)) = heap.pop() {
            if d > *dist.get(&pos).unwrap_or(&i32::MAX) {
                continue;
            }
            if pos == target {
                return d;
            }
            let (x, y) = pos;
            for &(_, dx, dy, _) in dirs.iter() {
                let nx = x + dx;
                let ny = y + dy;
                if let Some(&enter_cost) = cost_map.get(&(nx, ny)) {
                    let nd = d + enter_cost;
                    let entry = dist.entry((nx, ny)).or_insert(i32::MAX);
                    if nd < *entry {
                        *entry = nd;
                        heap.push((Reverse(nd), (nx, ny)));
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        // target was discovered but unreachable by Dijkstra (shouldn't happen), return -1
        -1
    }
}
```

Notes:
- The DFS physically moves the robot, so after exploring any branch we must move back (backtrack) to the previous cell — that's why we call move(opposite).
- We only record the entering cost of cells when we actually move into them; the start cell has no entering cost (we start there).
- After DFS we have a weighted graph of reachable cells only. Running Dijkstra on this graph gives the minimal total cost from the start to the target (summing costs of entered cells along the path).

This is the classic "Subarray Sum Equals K" problem. I'll explain the idea, walk through an example, give complexity, and then provide a clean C++ implementation.

Idea (prefix-sum + hash map):
- Let prefix[i] be the sum of the first i elements (prefix[0] = 0).
- The sum of subarray nums[l..r] equals prefix[r+1] - prefix[l].
- For a fixed r, we want to count how many l satisfy prefix[l] = prefix[r+1] - k.
- While scanning the array left to right, maintain a hash map that counts how many times each prefix sum has occurred so far.
  - Before processing any element, count[0] = 1 (empty prefix).
  - For each new prefix sum S, add count[S - k] (if present) to the answer, then increment count[S].
- This works in O(n) time and O(n) space, and correctly handles negative numbers.

Why it handles negative numbers:
- Negative numbers mean we cannot use two-pointer sliding window reliably; prefix-sum + map counts all valid left boundaries regardless of sign.

Example (nums = [1,1,1], k = 2):
- Start: count = {0:1}, prefix = 0, ans = 0
- Read 1: prefix = 1, need = 1 - 2 = -1, count[-1]=0 → ans += 0. Then count[1]++ → count = {0:1, 1:1}
- Read 1: prefix = 2, need = 2 - 2 = 0, count[0]=1 → ans += 1 (subarray [0..1]). Then count[2]++ → count={0:1,1:1,2:1}
- Read 1: prefix = 3, need = 3 - 2 = 1, count[1]=1 → ans += 1 (subarray [1..2]). Then count[3]++
- Final ans = 2.

Complexity:
- Time: O(n) — one pass through the array, hashmap operations average O(1).
- Space: O(n) — for the hashmap of prefix-sum frequencies.

C++ implementation:
- Uses long long for prefix sums to be safe.
- Uses unordered_map to store frequencies.
- Example main shows the two sample inputs.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int subarraySum(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    unordered_map<long long, int> count;
    count.reserve(nums.size() * 2);
    count[0] = 1; // empty prefix
    long long prefix = 0;
    int result = 0;
    for (int x : nums) {
        prefix += x;
        long long need = prefix - k;
        auto it = count.find(need);
        if (it != count.end()) result += it->second;
        ++count[prefix];
    }
    return result;
}

int main() {
    // Example 1
    vector<int> nums1 = {1,1,1};
    int k1 = 2;
    cout << "Example 1: " << subarraySum(nums1, k1) << "\n"; // expected 2

    // Example 2
    vector<int> nums2 = {1,2,3};
    int k2 = 3;
    cout << "Example 2: " << subarraySum(nums2, k2) << "\n"; // expected 2

    return 0;
}
```

Notes:
- If you prefer reading input from stdin for online judges, replace the main with code that reads n, the array, and k as required.
- Reserving space in the unordered_map can reduce rehashing overhead for large arrays.

You need to (1) find which column index has the given column label, then (2) read the cell at that row/column. wxGrid provides GetColLabelValue(col) and GetCellValue(row, col) for that.

Simple utility (safe checks + case-insensitive match):

#include <wx/grid.h>

wxString GetCellByColLabel(wxGrid* grid, const wxString& colLabel, int row)
{
    if (!grid) return wxEmptyString;
    if (row < 0 || row >= grid->GetNumberRows()) return wxEmptyString;

    for (int col = 0; col < grid->GetNumberCols(); ++col)
    {
        if (grid->GetColLabelValue(col).CmpNoCase(colLabel) == 0)
        {
            return grid->GetCellValue(row, col);
        }
    }

    // label not found
    return wxEmptyString;
}

Usage example:

wxString val = GetCellByColLabel(myGrid, "Age", 2);
if (!val.IsEmpty())
{
    // use val (note: empty string may mean either not found or empty cell)
}

Notes and alternatives:
- If you need to distinguish "label not found" vs "cell is empty", return a bool and use an out-parameter for the value.
- If you will do many lookups, build a map<string,int> from labels to column indexes once to avoid scanning columns each time.
- GetCellValue returns a wxString. If you need a number convert with ToLong/ToDouble (or wxAtoi, etc.).

Here's a compact JavaScript solution that checks for an element in an array and also handles cases where the search element is a different type (for example a string "3" vs numeric 3).

This version:
- first checks strict equality (arr.includes),
- and if not found and type-insensitive matching is allowed, falls back to comparing string forms (so "3" matches 3),
- works correctly with duplicate elements (duplicates don't affect existence checks).

Code:
```javascript
function existsInArray(arr, element, { coerceTypes = true } = {}) {
  // Strict check first
  if (arr.includes(element)) return true;

  if (!coerceTypes) return false;

  // Fallback: compare string forms to handle "3" vs 3, etc.
  return arr.some(item => String(item) === String(element));
}

// Example usage:
const arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1];
const search = "3";

console.log(existsInArray(arr, search));            // true (coerces types by default)
console.log(existsInArray(arr, search, { coerceTypes: false })); // false (strict type check)
```

Notes:
- Using String(item) === String(element) is a simple and safe way to handle common numeric/string mismatches. It will also match "null" with null, etc., so if you need stricter rules you could try numeric conversion (Number) only when both sides represent numbers, or use loose equality (item == element) with caution.

