You cannot pass a column name as a normal SQL parameter — it must be injected into the SQL string. To avoid SQL injection you must validate or quote the name before you put it into the query. Below are safe patterns for several engines. Replace table/column/timestamp names as needed.

Assumptions: table = my_table, timestamp column = created_at, start = 2020-02-10, end = 2020-02-15.

1) Plain (if you know the column name in advance)
- PostgreSQL (inclusive between dates; use char_length to count characters)
  SELECT *
  FROM my_table
  WHERE created_at BETWEEN '2020-02-10'::timestamp AND '2020-02-15'::timestamp
  ORDER BY char_length(description) DESC;

- If you want end-exclusive (common for date ranges):
  WHERE created_at >= '2020-02-10'::timestamp
    AND created_at <  ('2020-02-15'::date + interval '1 day')

2) PostgreSQL — safe dynamic column using format/quote_ident (plpgsql function)
  CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION select_between_order(p_col text, p_start date, p_end date)
  RETURNS SETOF my_table AS $$
  BEGIN
    -- validate column exists
    IF NOT EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM information_schema.columns
      WHERE table_schema = 'public'
        AND table_name = 'my_table'
        AND column_name = p_col
    ) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'invalid column name';
    END IF;

    RETURN QUERY EXECUTE format(
      'SELECT * FROM public.my_table
       WHERE created_at BETWEEN %L::timestamp AND %L::timestamp
       ORDER BY char_length(%I) DESC',
      p_start, p_end, p_col
    );
  END;
  $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

  -- call:
  SELECT * FROM select_between_order('description','2020-02-10','2020-02-15');

3) MySQL — prepared statement with a sanitized column name
  SET @col = 'description';
  -- escape any backticks in the incoming name
  SET @safe_col = REPLACE(@col, '`', '``');
  SET @sql = CONCAT(
    'SELECT * FROM my_table
     WHERE created_at BETWEEN ? AND ?
     ORDER BY CHAR_LENGTH(`', @safe_col, '`) DESC'
  );
  PREPARE stmt FROM @sql;
  SET @start = '2020-02-10';
  SET @end   = '2020-02-15';
  EXECUTE stmt USING @start, @end;
  DEALLOCATE PREPARE stmt;

4) SQL Server — dynamic SQL with QUOTENAME (prevents injection) and sp_executesql
  DECLARE @col sysname = N'description';
  IF NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM sys.columns
    WHERE object_id = OBJECT_ID(N'dbo.my_table') AND name = @col
  ) THROW 50000, 'Invalid column', 1;

  DECLARE @sql nvarchar(max) =
    N'SELECT * FROM dbo.my_table
      WHERE created_at BETWEEN @s AND @e
      ORDER BY LEN(CAST(' + QUOTENAME(@col) + N' AS nvarchar(max))) DESC';

  EXEC sp_executesql @sql,
       N'@s datetime, @e datetime',
       @s = '2020-02-10', @e = '2020-02-15';

Notes
- Use char_length() in Postgres, CHAR_LENGTH() in MySQL, LEN() in SQL Server (length() in some dialects counts bytes).
- Always validate the dynamic column against allowed names or use the DB identifier-quoting function (quote_ident / QUOTENAME) to avoid injection.
- Dates can be passed as parameters (sp_executesql / prepared statements) — only the column name requires dynamic SQL.

Here's a JavaScript function that implements the sorting rules you specified (case-sensitive, numbers before letters, if same starting letter: length descending, if same starting letter and length: ASCII descending, if still equal: preserve original order):

Notes:
- The comparator is stable because we attach original indices and use them as the final tiebreaker.
- "Numbers come before letters" is interpreted to mean: if one title starts with a digit and the other starts with a letter, the digit-starting title sorts earlier.
- Comparison of characters and whole strings is done using their code point (ASCII / Unicode code point) values, preserving case-sensitivity.

Code:

function sortBooks(books) {
  // preserve original indices to guarantee stability
  const arr = books.map((title, index) => ({ title, index }));

  const isDigit = c => c >= '0' && c <= '9';
  const isLetter = c => (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') || (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z');

  arr.sort((aObj, bObj) => {
    const a = aObj.title;
    const b = bObj.title;
    const ai = aObj.index;
    const bi = bObj.index;

    const aFirst = a.charAt(0);
    const bFirst = b.charAt(0);

    const aIsDigit = isDigit(aFirst);
    const bIsDigit = isDigit(bFirst);
    const aIsLetter = isLetter(aFirst);
    const bIsLetter = isLetter(bFirst);

    // Numbers should come before letters
    if (aIsDigit && bIsLetter) return -1;
    if (aIsLetter && bIsDigit) return 1;

    // Otherwise compare starting character by ASCII (case-sensitive)
    const aCode = aFirst.charCodeAt(0);
    const bCode = bFirst.charCodeAt(0);
    if (aCode !== bCode) return aCode - bCode;

    // Same starting character: sort by length descending
    if (a.length !== b.length) return b.length - a.length;

    // Same starting character and same length: sort by ASCII (whole string) descending
    if (a !== b) return a > b ? -1 : 1;

    // All equal: preserve original order (stable)
    return ai - bi;
  });

  return arr.map(x => x.title);
}

// Example usage:
const Books = [
  "3 Little Pigs",
  "Alice in Wonderland",
  "1 Fish 2 Fish Red Fish Blue Fish",
  "1984",
  "Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets"
];

console.log(sortBooks(Books));

